# Single Screen — "1 page = 1 screen"

The application targets users with little experience of information
systems: the essential information should be immediately visible and
easy to understand without unnecessary navigation or scrolling.

- Scroll behavior is defined exclusively in
  `knowledge-base/global/layout.mdc`.
- The main information must be visible and understandable at a glance,
  without requiring prior interaction.
- Dashboard pages should preserve a coherent single-screen view at `md+`.
- When the amount of information exceeds the available space, do not
  automatically extend the page. Organize, prioritize, condense, or
  progressively reveal secondary information while keeping the main view
  clear and coherent.
- The interface should preserve access to secondary information without
  unnecessarily breaking the user's context or forcing them to navigate
  to another page.
- Choose the organization pattern according to the content, hierarchy,
  interaction needs, and available space. Do not treat any specific
  component or pattern as mandatory.
- Possible patterns include, but are not limited to:
  - tabs or segmented views;
  - expandable or collapsible sections;
  - contextual panels or drawers;
  - modals;
  - pagination;
  - search and filtering;
  - compact data organization;
  - progressive disclosure;
  - component-level scrolling when permitted by the global layout rules;
  - or another existing Design System pattern that better preserves
    clarity and coherence.
- Prefer reusing existing components and established Design System
  patterns rather than introducing a new interaction pattern without need.
- Do not add interactions merely to avoid scrolling. The chosen pattern
  must make the information easier to understand and access.
- Prefer compact cards and tables and avoid large empty areas
  (see `knowledge-base/ux/information-density.mdc`).
- Use direct, simple vocabulary adapted to non-expert users
  (see `knowledge-base/ux/ui-copy.mdc`).

